cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
title: "transport-oxrsys: an unofficial OpenXR runtime for macOS"
abstract: >
  A fork of demonixis/oxrsys, an unofficial OpenXR runtime that a macOS
  application loads in place of the first-party runtime Apple does not ship. It
  renders through Metal, encodes the eye buffers, and streams them to a headset
  client over WiFi or USB; the headset returns head pose, hand joints and
  controller state. On the hexagon it is a transport layer: it terminates the
  protocol a headset speaks and triggers an interactor, and it depends on
  nothing further in. This copy carries work upstream does not, which is why the
  provenance is stated here rather than read off the fork flag.
authors:
  - family-names: Comte
    given-names: Yannick
    alias: demonixis
  - family-names: Lee
    given-names: "K. S. Ernest (iFire)"
    email: ernest.lee@chibifire.com
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4570-1436"
version: 1.2.0
license: MPL-2.0
repository-code: "https://github.com/v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric/transport-oxrsys"
keywords:
  - openxr
  - macos
  - metal
  - virtual-reality
  - runtime

references:

  # ── What this is a fork of ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
  - type: software
    title: "OXRSys Runtime: an unofficial OpenXR runtime"
    authors:
      - family-names: Comte
        given-names: Yannick
        alias: demonixis
    year: 2026
    version: 1.2.0
    license: MPL-2.0
    url: "https://github.com/demonixis/oxrsys"
    notes: >
      The whole of this tree except what this fork adds. MPL-2.0 is file-level
      copyleft: a modified file stays MPL-2.0 and the larger work does not have
      to be, which is what lets this sit beside MIT and Apache-2.0 code here.
      Upstream's README stays upstream's; Lib.mirrors/0 in infrastructure-logbook
      records demonixis/oxrsys as this repository's parent for that reason.

  # ── The specification it implements ──────────────────────────────────────────
  - type: software
    title: "OpenXR SDK"
    authors:
      - name: "The Khronos Group Inc."
    year: 2026
    version: release-1.1.57
    license: Apache-2.0
    url: "https://github.com/KhronosGroup/OpenXR-SDK"
    notes: >
      Fetched by CMake FetchContent, not vendored. Supplies the headers and the
      loader an application links, which is the surface this runtime is on the
      other side of. OpenXR is a trademark of The Khronos Group Inc., and this
      project is not affiliated with, endorsed by or approved by them.
  - type: software
    title: "OpenXR Conformance Test Suite"
    authors:
      - name: "The Khronos Group Inc."
    year: 2026
    license: Apache-2.0
    url: "https://github.com/KhronosGroup/OpenXR-CTS"
    notes: >
      An opt-in lane behind OXRSYS_ENABLE_CTS, off by default, fetched only when
      it is on. Upstream reports the pinned non-interactive baseline green as of
      2026-03-17: 63 passed, 36 skipped, 0 failed. Passing the CTS is not the
      same as being a conformant runtime, which is a Khronos adoption process
      this project has not been through.

  # ── Libraries the build fetches ──────────────────────────────────────────────
  - type: software
    title: "spdlog: fast C++ logging library"
    authors:
      - family-names: Melman
        given-names: Gabi
    year: 2026
    version: v1.15.3
    license: MIT
    url: "https://github.com/gabime/spdlog"
  - type: software
    title: "OpenGL Mathematics (GLM)"
    authors:
      - name: "G-Truc Creation"
    year: 2026
    version: 1.0.1
    license: MIT
    url: "https://github.com/g-truc/glm"
  - type: software
    title: "Catch2: a unit testing framework for C++"
    authors:
      - name: "Catch2 contributors"
    year: 2026
    version: v3.7.1
    license: BSL-1.0
    url: "https://github.com/catchorg/Catch2"
  - type: software
    title: "FFmpeg"
    authors:
      - name: "FFmpeg contributors"
    year: 2026
    license: LGPL-2.1-or-later
    url: "https://ffmpeg.org"
    notes: >
      The Linux encoder path and the Qt simulator's video preview only, found
      through pkg-config and linked dynamically against whatever the system
      installed. Never vendored and not on the macOS path, which encodes through
      VideoToolbox. An FFmpeg built with --enable-gpl is GPL-2.0-or-later and
      would be restricted under the third-party licence policy this workspace
      follows, so a build that redistributes binaries must check which one it
      linked rather than assume the default.

  # ── What is deliberately absent ──────────────────────────────────────────────
  - type: software
    title: "PSVR2 Linux Adapter Project"
    authors:
      - name: unterschall
    year: 2026
    license: GPL-2.0
    url: "https://github.com/unterschall/psvr2-linux-adapter"
    notes: >
      Not used, not vendored, and not linked. It is recorded because it is the
      obvious place to look for PlayStation VR2 support and the reason it is not
      here needs to be findable: it is a Linux kernel module, so it does not
      build on macOS at all, and it is GPL-2.0, which the licence policy calls
      restricted because it taints what links it. Merging it would take this
      MPL-2.0 tree with it. PSVR2 support for this runtime belongs in a separate
      repository that talks to this one across a process boundary, written from
      the published USB behaviour rather than from that source.
